+The scheduler's \emph{bookkeeping} is what it reads and writes on its own
+account, such as each process's value, status and latest run, and the
+releases at a kill. It goes through the bookkeeping interface. An operation
+there brings no \code{enter}, so nothing refuses it (\code{GRAPH.tex},
+Running and wrapping), and no refusal can hide a kill from the scheduler.
+What the scheduler does for a process, such as finding its code, goes
+through the top of the stack, where \code{protect} decides it.
 
